ontent":"Choosing the Right Format"},"4":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-2","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#1-text","content":"1. Text"},"5":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-3","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#2-binary","content":"2. Binary"},"6":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-4","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#3-cell","content":"3. Cell"},"7":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-5","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#how-to-request-a-signature-via-tonconnect","content":"How to Request a Signature via TonConnect"},"8":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-6","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#how-the-signature-is-built","content":"How the Signature Is Built"},"9":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-7","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"For `text` and `binary`"},"10":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-8","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#for-cell","content":"For `cell`"},"11":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-9","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#how-to-verify-the-signature","content":"How to Verify the Signature"},"12":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-10","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#in-javascript-off-chain","content":"In JavaScript (off-chain)"},"13":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-11","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"In a Smart Contract (on-chain)"},"14":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-12","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#tldr","content":"TL;DR"},"15":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-13","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/sign-data#useful-links","content":"Useful Links"},"16":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-14","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data","content":"When it comes to critical actions, a simple button press just doesn’t cut it. Real consent feels like your wallet standing tall and declaring: *\"Yes, it’s me! And I approve this!\"*"},"17":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-15","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data","content":"A signature is the real deal: cryptographic, verifiable, secure. You can sign anything — text, bytes, or cells. The signature can be verified off-chain in your dApp or passed into a smart contract for on-chain logic."},"18":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-16","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#what-is-signdata-and-why-it-matters","content":"Any action where the user must **consciously and explicitly** confirm their intent – like updating public profile information, linking a new email or phone number, or accepting DAO terms – deserves something more robust than just a click."},"19":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-17","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#what-is-signdata-and-why-it-matters","content":"**SignData** lets your dApp request a cryptographic signature – from the user's wallet – on arbitrary content. The user sees exactly what they are signing and can either approve or decline. The signature is generated using the same private key that signs TON transactions, but it provides no direct access to funds."},"20":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-18","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#what-is-signdata-and-why-it-matters","content":"For a dApp, this signature is a way to secure critical actions, verify a user's real intent, and – if needed – pass validated data into a smart contract for on-chain use."},"21":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-19","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#what-is-signdata-and-why-it-matters","content":"If you don’t clearly get what an **Cell**, **TL-B**, or **public key** is? Read the glossary below"},"22":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-20","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#what-is-signdata-and-why-it-matters","content":"**TonConnect**  –  The protocol connecting your dApp to the user's wallet. It handles connections, transaction requests, and data signing. \n**SignData**  –  A TonConnect method that allows a dApp to request the user to sign arbitrary data (text, bytes, or Cell) with their wallet. No transaction involved – just a signature.\n**Cell**  –  The atomic unit of data in TON. Holds up to 1023 bits and 4 references to other cells. Everything in TON – accounts, messages, blocks – is made of Cells. Each Cell has a hash that acts as its unique ID.\n**TL-B schema**  –  Declarative definition of Cell structure. It’s required for wallets to display signing details and for smart contracts to validate the content.\n**Ed25519**  –  The signing algorithm used in TON. It powers both transactions and `signData`. Everything signed by the user uses Ed25519 over a SHA256 hash of a strictly formatted message.\n**Address**  –  The account address in TON. Formed by a `workchain` (usually `0`) and a hash of the contract’s code and initialization data. For standard wallets, this hash is deterministically derived from the public key.\n**Public Key**  –  The public part of the user’s key pair, derived from the private key. Used to verify signatures. In TON, the public key becomes available on-chain only after the wallet is initialized (i.e., has sent at least one transaction). Until then, it’s known only to the dApp via TonConnect and the user."},"23":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-21","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#1-text","content":"Use this when the data is human-readable."},"24":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-22","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#1-text","content":"**Pros:**"},"25":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-23","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#1-text","content":"The user sees exactly what they are signing."},"26":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-24","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#1-text","content":"Simple to implement."},"27":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-25","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#1-text","content":"Perfect for off-chain confirmations."},"28":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-26","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#1-text","content":"**Example:**"},"29":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-27","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#2-binary","content":"Use this when signing a hash, arbitrary bytes, or a file."},"30":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-28","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#2-binary","content":"**Pros:**"},"31":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-29","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#2-binary","content":"Useful for generating digital receipts, hashed content references, etc."},"32":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-30","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#2-binary","content":"Works when the content isn’t human-readable or shouldn't be shown."},"33":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-31","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#2-binary","content":"**Example:**"},"34":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-32","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#3-cell","content":"Use this if the signed data should be **verifiable and restorable inside a smart contract**."},"35":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-33","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#3-cell","content":"**Pros:**"},"36":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-34","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#3-cell","content":"Smart contracts can validate the signature during execution."},"37":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-35","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#3-cell","content":"Supports structured data via TL-B schemas."},"38":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-36","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#3-cell","content":"**Example:**"},"39":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-37","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#3-cell","content":"**Clarification on Root Type**: If the TL-B schema provided in SignData.cell.schema defines multiple types (for example, one primary type along with auxiliary helper types), the last type definition in the schema is treated as the root type for serialization and deserialization. In practice, this means the cell’s content is parsed as an instance of the final type declared in the schema."},"40":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-38","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#how-to-request-a-signature-via-tonconnect","content":"TonConnect handles the request and shows the prompt in the user’s wallet. They either sign or decline."},"41":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-39","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#how-to-request-a-signature-via-tonconnect","content":"**You receive:**"},"42":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-40","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"The message is constructed:"},"43":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-41","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"Components:"},"44":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-42","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"`Address`: wallet address (workchain + hash)"},"45":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-43","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"`AppDomain`: dApp domain as UTF-8 string with length"},"46":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-44","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"`Timestamp`: time of signing"},"47":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-45","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"`Payload`:"},"48":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-46","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"Prefix `txt` / `bin`"},"49":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-47","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"Length"},"50":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-48","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"Text (utf-8 encoded) or bytes"},"51":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-49","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-text-and-binary","content":"Then:"},"52":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-50","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-cell","content":"A Cell is built:"},"53":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-51","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-cell","content":"The hash of this Cell is signed:"},"54":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-52","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#for-cell","content":"🔥 Example signing in JS: https://github.com/mois-ilya/ton-sign-data-reference"},"55":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-53","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-javascript-off-chain","content":"For `text` and `binary`, you can reconstruct the message:"},"56":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-54","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-javascript-off-chain","content":"Rebuild the byte array exactly as described above."},"57":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-55","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-javascript-off-chain","content":"Retrieve the public key (from TonConnect or known in advance)."},"58":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-56","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-javascript-off-chain","content":"Verify:"},"59":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-57","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-javascript-off-chain","content":"**Important:** Always ensure that `result.address` matches the wallet you requested the signature from. Otherwise, an attacker could swap in a valid signature from a different wallet."},"60":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-58","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-javascript-off-chain","content":"🔥 Example verification in JS: https://github.com/mois-ilya/ton-sign-data-reference"},"61":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-59","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"For the `cell` format, smart contracts can verify the signature directly."},"62":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-60","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"They must validate:"},"63":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-61","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"Prefix: `0x75569022`"},"64":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-62","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"Schema hash matches"},"65":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-63","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"Timestamp is recent"},"66":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-64","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"Address matches"},"67":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-65","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"App domain matches"},"68":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-66","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"Signature is valid using `ed25519`\n📄  check_signature in standard library"},"69":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-67","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#in-a-smart-contract-on-chain","content":"🔥 On-chain example in FunC: https://github.com/p0lunin/sign-data-contract-verify-example"},"70":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-68","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#tldr","content":"The **SignData** method in TonConnect is a secure way for a dApp to request a user’s cryptographic signature on text, bytes, or TON cells – directly through their wallet. It’s **not a transaction**, but a confirmation that can be verified off-chain or on-chain."},"71":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-69","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#tldr","content":"**Users see what they sign** (if it's text or a proper TL-B schema)."},"72":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-70","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#tldr","content":"**Signature uses the same key as for transactions**, but doesn’t send anything."},"73":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-71","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#tldr","content":"**Verification works anywhere** – on your backend or inside a smart contract."},"74":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-72","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#tldr","content":"| Format | What’s Signed          | Wallet UI       | Use Case                        |\n| ------ | ---------------------- | --------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `text` | Human-readable message | Displayed as-is | Action confirmation, agreements |","table":"table-0"},"75":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-73","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#tldr","content":"| Format   | What’s Signed                | Wallet UI     | Use Case                |\n| -------- | ---------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------- |\n| `binary` | Bytes / hashes / identifiers | Shows warning | Document hashes, TX IDs |","table":"table-0"},"76":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-74","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#tldr","content":"| Format | What’s Signed         | Wallet UI                   | Use Case                  |\n| ------ | --------------------- | --------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `cell` | Cell with TL-B schema | Schema + warning or content | Smart contract validation |","table":"table-0"},"77":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-75","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#tldr","content":"If you’re building a dApp that needs real user consent and want to verify it without extra hassle – SignData gets the job done."},"78":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-76","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#useful-links","content":"**Demo dApp to test `signData`**  \n  tonkeeper.github.io/demo-dapp-with-wallet"},"79":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-77","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#useful-links","content":"**JS signature verification example**  \n  github.com/mois-ilya/ton-sign-data-reference"},"80":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-78","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#useful-links","content":"**Smart contract verification example**  \n  github.com/p0lunin/sign-data-contract-verify-example"},"81":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-79","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#useful-links","content":"**Keeper’s SignData spec**  \n  github.com/tonkeeper/ton-connect-docs – SignData"},"82":{"id":"/academy/sign-data-80","page_id":"/academy/sign-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/sign-data#useful-links","content":"**FunC `check_signature` standard lib**  \n  docs.ton.org – check_signature"},"83":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"page","content":"Tracking TON Transactions: From Message to Trace","breadcrumbs":["Tonconsole","Academy"],"tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking"},"84":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-0","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#what-has-a-unique-id-and-what-doesnt","content":"What Has a Unique ID and What Doesn’t?"},"85":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-1","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#the-tldr","content":"The TL;DR"},"86":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-2","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#how-external-in-normalization-works","content":"How External-in Normalization Works"},"87":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-3","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#library-usage-examples","content":"Library Usage Examples"},"88":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-4","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#tracking-a-transaction-by-normalized-hash","content":"Tracking a Transaction by Normalized Hash"},"89":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-5","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#key-rest-endpoints","content":"Key REST Endpoints"},"90":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-6","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#polling-strategy","content":"Polling Strategy"},"91":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-7","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#trace-lifecycle-states","content":"Trace Lifecycle States"},"92":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-8","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#example-of-isfinalized-function","content":"Example of `isFinalized` function"},"93":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-9","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#minimal-integration-flow","content":"Minimal Integration Flow"},"94":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-10","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#if-youre-building-a-dapp","content":"If You’re Building a dApp"},"95":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-11","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#if-youre-building-something-else","content":"If You’re Building Something Else"},"96":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-12","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"Alright — let’s break down how the hell you’re supposed to track a transaction in TON. If you’ve ever used Ethereum or Bitcoin, you might think: _“Yeah, I just grab the hash of the transaction and I’m good.”_ Well, surprise: TON doesn’t work that way. Not even close."},"97":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-13","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"If you don’t clearly get what an **account**, **transaction**, **message**, **trace**, **cell**, or **cell-hash** is — or what the wallet actually signs — buckle up and read the glossary."},"98":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-14","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"**Account** – Basic unit of information storage in the blockchain; a record in the workchain state containing metadata, balance, and possibly code and data. \n**Messages** – Data packets exchanged between accounts (internal) or between accounts and entities outside the blockchain (external-in or external-out). \n**Transactions** – Atomic changes to an account’s state. Typically (excluding system transactions), they're reactions to incoming messages. The exact validator processing time can't be predetermined, thus neither the timestamp nor hash can be known in advance. \n*(Transactions can shoot out internal messages, which go to other accounts, triggering more transactions, and so on – it’s like digital dominoes.)*\n**Trace** – It’s a tree. Nodes = transactions. Edges = messages. The root? Usually a response to an external-in.\nWant examples? Go check tonviewer.com – some are simple, others branch out beautifully, and some are just degenerate one-offs. \n\n**Cell** – The atomic unit in TON. Up to 1023 bits and up to 4 refs to other cells. Everything lives in cells – accounts, messages, transactions, even blocks. And each cell has its own hash. That hash is how you track stuff.\n**Hash** – Anytime you see *\"hash\"* here, it means the hash of the cell. Don't try to hash the raw bytes like a savage. It won’t work."},"99":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-15","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"Simple Example"},"100":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-16","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"You send your buddy 5 GRAM using Keeper.\nBehind the scenes, your wallet puts together an instruction for your account contract, which then runs something like this:"},"101":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-17","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"Make sure the instruction isn't too old (check the TTL)."},"102":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-18","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"Check that the sequence number (`seqno`) is one higher than the last one."},"103":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-19","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"Verify the signature against the public key stored in the account."},"104":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-20","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"Take the draft of the outgoing internal message (the 5 GRAM transfer) from the external message body, fill in the actual sender info, and send it out."},"105":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-21","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"This whole thing – the instruction and signature – goes into the **body** of the external-in message. But here’s the weird part: the message **headers** (like the recipient address) aren’t signed at all. They just specify which account should receive the message, and off it goes to the blockchain."},"106":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-22","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking","content":"In decoded form, it looks something like this:\n\nOnce it lands, two transactions and two messages are recorded on-chain. The external-in message from the wallet triggers the sender’s transaction, which emits an internal message. That internal message reaches the recipient and triggers their transaction."},"107":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-23","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#what-has-a-unique-id-and-what-doesnt","content":"**Account** – It’s globally unique. Workchain + 256-bit address. Easy."},"108":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-24","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#what-has-a-unique-id-and-what-doesnt","content":"**Transaction** – Unique per account and `lt`. So `AccountID + lt` = unique. And that’s in the body, so the hash is globally unique too."},"109":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-25","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#what-has-a-unique-id-and-what-doesnt","content":"**Messages (internal + external-out)** – Even in the same transaction, they'll have different `lt`. That plus source account = unique hash.\n_(Tiny exception: some weird old system messages might break this. Don’t worry about it unless you’re working at the protocol level.)_"},"110":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-26","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#what-has-a-unique-id-and-what-doesnt","content":"**External-in** – This one's a bit tricky. A single external-in message can end up creating multiple transactions – for example, if the wallet didn't set the +2 flag and the account doesn't have enough funds. In such cases, the message will keep being retried until the TTL expires or the account runs out of money. So by default, external-in messages are **not** globally unique.\nBUT – if you’re using a standard wallet contract (like v3r1–v5r1) and sets the correct flags (which most popular wallets do), then the **message body + sender address** combination becomes unique. That means the message hash is globally unique and can be used as an ID right after signing – even before the blockchain sees it."},"111":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-27","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#what-has-a-unique-id-and-what-doesnt","content":"**Trace** – Usually tracked by the root transaction hash. Explorers like tonviewer.com will let you find a trace from any globally unique message or transaction hash."},"112":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-28","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#the-tldr","content":"Only one identifier exists **before** the validator touches anything: the hash of the **external-in message**.\nBut there are a **few things** you need to understand and account for:"},"113":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-29","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#the-tldr","content":"The wallet contract that sends the transaction must behave correctly."},"114":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-30","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#the-tldr","content":"This only works for wallets that support `seqno` and `ttl` – older or non-standard contracts won't do."},"115":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-31","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#the-tldr","content":"Only the message body is signed – the rest (headers like destination) is not. So relayers or other middleware might change those parts before the message gets into the blockchain, changing the final hash. That’s why normalization is needed."},"116":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-32","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#the-tldr","content":"You can’t query a message by its hash using a light server – they don’t index that. You’ll need a full blockchain index (like tonapi.io)."},"117":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-33","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#how-external-in-normalization-works","content":"Because relayers can modify parts of a message before it hits the chain, we normalize the message to get a stable, predictable hash. Here's how it works:"},"118":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-34","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#how-external-in-normalization-works","content":"Extract the original message from the cell."},"119":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-35","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#how-external-in-normalization-works","content":"Keep only two things: the destination address and the message body."},"120":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-36","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#how-external-in-normalization-works","content":"Repack the message with the body stored as a reference – this guarantees a consistent structure."},"121":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-37","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#how-external-in-normalization-works","content":"Store it in a new, empty cell and compute the hash."},"122":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-38","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#how-external-in-normalization-works","content":"For a more detailed, step-by-step example of tracking a transaction using normalized hashes, check out the Cookbook."},"123":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-39","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#tracking-a-transaction-by-normalized-hash","content":"A **normalized hash** can be resolved **only** by full blockchain indexers (such as tonapi, toncenter, or dton). Lite servers and archive nodes do not understand it."},"124":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-40","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#tracking-a-transaction-by-normalized-hash","content":"The examples below use **TONAPI**"},"125":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-41","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#key-rest-endpoints","content":"These are the main endpoints that accept a normalized message hash and allow you to determine whether the operation was accepted on-chain, as well as retrieve related data such as the transaction, execution trace, or event."},"126":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-42","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#key-rest-endpoints","content":"| Goal                                            | Endpoint   |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Get the full trace (transaction execution tree) | `getTrace` |","table":"table-0"},"127":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-43","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#key-rest-endpoints","content":"| Goal                               | Endpoint   |\n| ---------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Get a high-level event aggregating | `getEvent` |","table":"table-0"},"128":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-44","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#key-rest-endpoints","content":"| Goal                                       | Endpoint           |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------ |\n| Get Jetton-related transfers from an event | `getJettonsEvents` |","table":"table-0"},"129":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-45","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#key-rest-endpoints","content":"| Goal                               | Endpoint                   |\n| ---------------------------------- | -------------------------- |\n| Get a root transaction by its hash | `getBlockchainTransaction` |","table":"table-0"},"130":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-46","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#key-rest-endpoints","content":"| Goal                                             | Endpoint                                |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------- |\n| Get the transaction that consumed a specific msg | `getBlockchainTransactionByMessageHash` |","table":"table-0"},"131":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-47","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#key-rest-endpoints","content":"For the purposes of this guide we will focus on **getTrace**, because it captures the status of the entire transaction chain and therefore lets us determine whether the operation succeeded."},"132":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-48","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#polling-strategy","content":"Polling interval: `1s → 2s → 4s → 8s → …` (exponential backoff)"},"133":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-49","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#polling-strategy","content":"If the external message includes an `expire_time` field — use it as a hard cutoff. Once the current time exceeds `expire_time`, the message will never be accepted by the network. Stop polling and mark the attempt as failed."},"134":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-50","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#polling-strategy","content":"If the message has no `expire_time`, use a custom upper limit (e.g. 60–120 seconds) depending on your UX needs."},"135":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-51","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#trace-lifecycle-states","content":"| Stage         | API response                          | Meaning                                                                        |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| **Not found** | `GET /v2/traces/{trace_id}` → **404** | The message has not yet reached any validator (it is not even in the mempool). |","table":"table-1"},"136":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-52","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#trace-lifecycle-states","content":"| Stage       | API response                                                                           | Meaning                                                                                              |\n| ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Pending** | **200 OK** with a Trace object, but `isFinalized(trace) == false` (see function below) | The message is in the mempool or partially executed, but the full transaction chain is not complete. |","table":"table-1"},"137":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-53","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#trace-lifecycle-states","content":"| Stage         | API response                                | Meaning                                                             |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Finalized** | **200 OK** and `isFinalized(trace) == true` | The entire transaction chain has finished; the result is immutable. |","table":"table-1"},"138":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-54","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#trace-lifecycle-states","content":"> **Tip:** cache `{trace_id, expiresAt}` locally.\n> If the trace is still _Not found_ or _Pending_ after `expiresAt`, mark the operation as failed and prompt the user to retry."},"139":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-55","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#example-of-isfinalized-function","content":"A trace is finalized when:"},"140":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-56","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#example-of-isfinalized-function","content":"it is not emulated"},"141":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-57","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#example-of-isfinalized-function","content":"it contains no outgoing internal messages"},"142":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-58","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#example-of-isfinalized-function","content":"all child traces are finalized as well"},"143":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-59","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#minimal-integration-flow","content":"**After signing** — compute the normalized hash from the BOC the wallet returns."},"144":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-60","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#minimal-integration-flow","content":"**Start polling** `traces/{hash}` with the back‑off scheme above."},"145":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-61","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#minimal-integration-flow","content":"**When “pending” appears** — present the explorer link or custom “Pending” badge."},"146":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-62","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#minimal-integration-flow","content":"**When finalized** — parse the trace:"},"147":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-63","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#minimal-integration-flow","content":"All transactions `success` → show confirmation."},"148":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-64","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#minimal-integration-flow","content":"Any error → show failure reason (bounce, low balance, etc.)."},"149":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-65","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#minimal-integration-flow","content":"**Stop** once success or definitive failure is recorded."},"150":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-66","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#minimal-integration-flow","content":"This keeps the user informed from the moment a message leaves the wallet until the entire domino chain of transactions is settled on‑chain."},"151":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-67","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#if-youre-building-a-dapp","content":"When the user sends a message through TonConnect, you receive a **BOC** (Bag of Cells) — an encoded copy of the sent message. Normalize it, calculate the hash, and boom – you can show an explorer link right away or track what happens next."},"152":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-68","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#if-youre-building-a-dapp","content":"This BOC contains everything you need to get the exact same normalized hash, even before the message appears on-chain. See how to decode and handle BOC strings in the Cookbook."},"153":{"id":"/academy/transaction-tracking-69","page_id":"/academy/transaction-tracking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/academy/transaction-tracking#if-youre-building-something-else","content":"You should probably go read the full article: original here [ru]. It’s got all the gritty, boring details we skipped here."},"154":{"id":"/","page_id":"/","type":"page","content":"Welcome to Documentation","breadcrumbs":["Tonconsole"],"tags":[],"url":"/"},"155":{"id":"/-0","page_id":"/","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/#value-proposal","content":"Value proposal"},"156":{"id":"/-1","page_id":"/","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/#additional-services","content":"Additional services"},"157":{"id":"/-2","page_id":"/","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"Find the technical documentation and guides for Ton API, Ton Console, Keeper and more. Launch projects, use network, enhance your business in the ecosystem of TON blockchain."},"158":{"id":"/-3","page_id":"/","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/#value-proposal","content":"Tonconsole — B2B platform for developers and product managers. Create new features, manage your business on TON, use TON API."},"159":{"id":"/-4","page_id":"/","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/#value-proposal","content":"Tonviewer — observe transactions on TON blockchain."},"160":{"id":"/-5","page_id":"/","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/#value-proposal","content":"Keeper — B2C app with 12M+ MAU and dapp browser. Get the traffic for your products."},"161":{"id":"/-6","page_id":"/","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/#additional-services","content":"Jettons whitelist"},"162":{"id":"/-7","page_id":"/","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/#additional-services","content":"<Card title=\"Read docs\" href=\"/tonapi\" />"},"163":{"id":"/-8","page_id":"/","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/#additional-services","content":"<Card title=\"Open TonConsole\" external href=\"https://tonconsole.com/\" />"},"164":{"id":"/-9","page_id":"/","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/#additional-services","content":"<Card title=\"Chat with team\" external href=\"LINKS.CONTACT\" />"},"165":{"id":"/payment-processing","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"page","content":"Payment Processing","breadcrumbs":["Tonconsole","Payment Processing"],"tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing"},"166":{"id":"/payment-processing-0","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#key-features-by-processing-approach","content":"Key Features by Processing Approach"},"167":{"id":"/payment-processing-1","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#asset-types","content":"Asset Types"},"168":{"id":"/payment-processing-2","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#deployment-and-maintenance","content":"Deployment and Maintenance"},"169":{"id":"/payment-processing-3","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#interaction-with-blockchain","content":"Interaction with Blockchain"},"170":{"id":"/payment-processing-4","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#payment-acceptance-methods","content":"Payment Acceptance Methods"},"171":{"id":"/payment-processing-5","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#interaction-interfaces","content":"Interaction Interfaces"},"172":{"id":"/payment-processing-6","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#source-code-options","content":"Source Code Options"},"173":{"id":"/payment-processing-7","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#trust-level-in-blockchain-interaction","content":"Trust Level in Blockchain Interaction"},"174":{"id":"/payment-processing-8","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#tonapi-embed-solution","content":"TonAPI Embed Solution"},"175":{"id":"/payment-processing-9","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#feedback-and-customer-needs","content":"Feedback and Customer Needs"},"176":{"id":"/payment-processing-10","page_id":"/payment-processing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing#contact-us","content":"Contact Us"},"177":{"id":"/payment-processing-11","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing","content":"Our payment processing solutions for the TON network are designed to meet a variety of needs, offering customizable options to suit specific requirements. These solutions are built to handle high payment volumes, making them suitable even for CEX-scale products, and are already trusted by CEXes and other clients."},"178":{"id":"/payment-processing-12","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing","content":"> **Disclaimer**  \n> The solutions' availability or use may be subject to local regulatory requirements. The users are responsible for ensuring their own compliance with applicable laws.\n> The solutions are technology-based and do not constitute regulated payment services, e-money issuance, or cryptoasset exchange services .\n> The offered solutions  do not take  custody of user funds or private keys."},"179":{"id":"/payment-processing-13","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#asset-types","content":"**GRAM** (native token of the TON network)"},"180":{"id":"/payment-processing-14","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#asset-types","content":"**Jettons**"},"181":{"id":"/payment-processing-15","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#deployment-and-maintenance","content":"**Self-hosted** solutions for full control over sensitive data and risk mitigation."},"182":{"id":"/payment-processing-16","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#deployment-and-maintenance","content":"**Access to auxiliary services**, such as liteservers, ensures reliability and stability."},"183":{"id":"/payment-processing-17","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#interaction-with-blockchain","content":"**Monitoring and indexing** of payment-related transactions without private key storage."},"184":{"id":"/payment-processing-18","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#interaction-with-blockchain","content":"**Message assembly and signing** by a trusted party (optional)."},"185":{"id":"/payment-processing-19","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#interaction-with-blockchain","content":"**Full-fledged payment processor** for both monitoring and performing transfers."},"186":{"id":"/payment-processing-20","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#payment-acceptance-methods","content":"Payments to **one or a few contracts (wallets)** with a memo."},"187":{"id":"/payment-processing-21","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#payment-acceptance-methods","content":"Payments to **individual deposit addresses** for each user (no memo required)."},"188":{"id":"/payment-processing-22","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#interaction-interfaces","content":"**REST API**"},"189":{"id":"/payment-processing-23","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#interaction-interfaces","content":"Customizable or newly developed interfaces based on customer requirements."},"190":{"id":"/payment-processing-24","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#source-code-options","content":"**Closed-source** solutions"},"191":{"id":"/payment-processing-25","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#source-code-options","content":"**Open-source** solutions with our support."},"192":{"id":"/payment-processing-26","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#trust-level-in-blockchain-interaction","content":"We use the **liteapi interface**, provided by liteservers, to connect offchain services with the TON blockchain. To ensure trust and prevent data falsification by liteservers, our solutions include proof-check mechanisms, supporting trustless communication with both public and private liteservers."},"193":{"id":"/payment-processing-27","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#tonapi-embed-solution","content":"**Demo API in testnet**"},"194":{"id":"/payment-processing-28","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#tonapi-embed-solution","content":"**Documentation page**"},"195":{"id":"/payment-processing-29","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#feedback-and-customer-needs","content":"Share your feedback via Google Forms."},"196":{"id":"/payment-processing-30","page_id":"/payment-processing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing#contact-us","content":"**Telegram:** Support Bot"},"197":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"page","content":"TonAPI Embed","breadcrumbs":["Tonconsole","Payment Processing"],"tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed"},"198":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-0","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#demo-instance","content":"Demo instance"},"199":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-1","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"Glossary"},"200":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-2","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#description-of-the-current-service-functionality","content":"Description of the current service functionality"},"201":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-3","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#api-methods","content":"API methods"},"202":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-4","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"GET Transfers history"},"203":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-5","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfer-by-hash","content":"GET Transfer by Hash"},"204":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-6","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-balance","content":"GET Balance"},"205":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-7","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#add-tracking-address","content":"Add tracking Address"},"206":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-8","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#resolve-domain","content":"Resolve domain"},"207":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-9","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#status","content":"Status"},"208":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-10","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#methods-for-withdrawals","content":"Methods for withdrawals"},"209":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-11","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","content":"A service for indexing blockchain data related to the transfers of funds (GRAM and Jettons).\nDesigned for self-hosted use and integration into the infrastructure of CEX and services that process deposits and withdrawals on the TON blockchain.\nThe service requires a connection to one or more nodes of the TON network."},"210":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-12","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#demo-instance","content":"https://demo-embed.tonapi.io/"},"211":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-13","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Message*** - A message is a packet of data sent between actors (users, applications, smart contracts) on TON blockchain. https://docs.ton.org/develop/smart-contracts/guidelines/message-delivery-guarantees"},"212":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-14","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Jetton*** - fungible token on the TON blockchain."},"213":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-15","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Wallet smart contract*** - specialized smart contract designed specifically for wallet apps and to be used by end users."},"214":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-16","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Deposit address*** - address in TON blockchain to which funds can be received and from which funds can be withdrawn."},"215":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-17","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Deposit smart contract*** - a smart contract on the TON blockchain that can hold the balance of some asset (GRAM or Jetton). It is usually (but not always) a ***Wallet smart contract.*** The address of the recipient is not always the same as the address of the smart contract where the asset is actually stored. For example, for Jettons, the recipient address is the smart contract address of the Jetton wallet owner."},"216":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-18","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Transaction*** - low-level entity in the TON network. A transaction is an operation that processes inbound and can create outbound messages related to a specific account, altering its state and potentially generating fees for validators. https://docs.ton.org/develop/data-formats/transaction-layout"},"217":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-19","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Transfer*** - abstract entity describing the movement of funds between ***Deposit smart contracts.*** Contains information about the sender, recipient, amount, etc. This information can be derived from several low-level entities in the TON blockchain, such as ***Messages*** and ***Transactions***."},"218":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-20","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Centralized deposit address*** - a ***Deposit address*** to receive funds from many users. Usually requires the use of a memo to accept payments to this address."},"219":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-21","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Individual deposit address*** - a ***Deposit address*** to receive funds from one user. The use of a memo is not required."},"220":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-22","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Withdrawal*** - the action of sending funds from one ***Deposit smart contract*** to another by sending a ***Message*** to the TON blockchain."},"221":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-23","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Liteserver Node*** - a node in the TON network that provides an API for external services to interact with TON."},"222":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-24","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Archive Node* - *Liteserver Node*** that stores historical blockchain data**.**"},"223":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-25","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Private key*** - ***Wallet smart contract*** private key or seed phrase for signing withdrawal ***Message***. One private key can manage several wallets."},"224":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-26","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***TEP74*** - standard for fungible tokens in the TON blockchain. https://github.com/ton-blockchain/TEPs/blob/master/text/0074-jettons-standard.md"},"225":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-27","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***LT*** (logical time) - blockchain specific and account specific counter to organize messages and transactions monotonically increases (but contains gaps) for any given account (https://docs.ton.org/develop/smart-contracts/guidelines/message-delivery-g)"},"226":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-28","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Jetton address*** (Jetton master contract address) - for better scaling, the Jetton contract (master contract) in the TON blockchain does not store information about the users' balance, but only general information about the token itself. User balance information is stored by a separate smart wallet token contract. Which in our case is considered a deposit.\nWhen we talk about a particular ***Jetton*** in general, we identify it by the address of the master contract. https://docs.ton.org/develop/dapps/asset-processing/jettons#jetton-architecture"},"227":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-29","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Fee*** - fee charged in GRAM by the TON network when processing a transaction. It has a complex structure and depends on a large number of factors. In this regard, it is difficult to predict the exact size of the commission and to calculate the sum value. https://docs.ton.org/develop/smart-contracts/fees"},"228":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-30","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Minimum indivisible units*** - a measure of the quantity of GRAM or ***Jettons*** in which the quantity is always an integer. For GRAM, it is nanoGRAM. The position of the decimal point when converting an amount of ***Minimum indivisible units*** is determined by the `decimals` Jetton parameter."},"229":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-31","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#glossary","content":"***Masterchain block*** - Block of Masterchain. The Masterchain is the primary chain that stores the network configuration and the final state of all workchains. You can understand this as the masterchain being the core directory, a single source of truth for all the shards in the ecosystem. This block is selected as a synchronization point to refer to a specific smart contract state."},"230":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-32","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#description-of-the-current-service-functionality","content":"The service is designed to track deposits and withdrawals at one or several ***Centralized deposit addresses*** for GRAM and Jettons."},"231":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-33","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#description-of-the-current-service-functionality","content":"The API structure, composition and format of returned data can be changed upon customer's request."},"232":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-34","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#description-of-the-current-service-functionality","content":"The service requires access to one or more ***Liteserver Nodes/Archive Nodes*** (not necessarily trusted ones, since the service has proof check)."},"233":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-35","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#description-of-the-current-service-functionality","content":"The service provides a REST API with a set of methods to track the movement of funds on a given pool of ***Deposit addresses***."},"234":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-36","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#description-of-the-current-service-functionality","content":"Service scans ***Transfers*** until the moment of ***Deposit smart contract*** creation, therefore, when scanning ***Deposit smart contract*** with a long history you will need access to the ***Archive node***."},"235":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-37","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#description-of-the-current-service-functionality","content":"Specific service performance and health metrics can be added on request."},"236":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-38","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#api-methods","content":"You can get Swagger file here: **Swagger file** \nYou can use Swagger UI to explore the API methods: **Swagger UI**"},"237":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-39","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"`GET /account/{address}/history` \n`GET /jetton/{jetton}/{address}/history`"},"238":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-40","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"Methods for obtaining the ***Transfer*** history (with pagination) for a specific ***Deposit address***."},"239":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-41","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"Returns the following data:"},"240":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-42","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"Address of sender and recipients"},"241":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-43","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"***Jetton address***"},"242":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-44","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"Amount of transferred GRAM and Jettons in ***Minimum indivisible units*** of measurement"},"243":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-45","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"Unix timestamp"},"244":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-46","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"***LT*** (logical time)"},"245":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-47","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"Hash of ***Transaction*** (as a unique identifier for this transfer)"},"246":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-48","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"Text comment from the ***Message*** (if any)"},"247":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-49","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfers-history","content":"***Fee***"},"248":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-50","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfer-by-hash","content":"`GET /account/{address}/history/{hash}` \n`GET /jetton/{jetton}/{address}/history/{hash}`"},"249":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-51","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-transfer-by-hash","content":"Methods for obtaining a specific ***Transfer*** by ***Transaction*** hash."},"250":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-52","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-balance","content":"`GET /account/{address}` \n`GET /jetton/{jetton}/{address}`"},"251":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-53","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-balance","content":"Methods for obtaining the ***Deposit smart contract*** balance (GRAM or Jetton) for the last ***Masterchain block*** known to the service."},"252":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-54","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-balance","content":"The last known ***Masterchain block*** depends on the synchronization of the node with the TON blockchain."},"253":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-55","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-balance","content":"Returns the following data:"},"254":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-56","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-balance","content":"Balance in ***Minimum indivisible units*** of measurement"},"255":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-57","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-balance","content":"***Masterchain block*** seqno"},"256":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-58","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-balance","content":"Last ***LT*** - identifies the last known ***Transaction*** on the ***Deposit smart contract***"},"257":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-59","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#get-balance","content":"Last processed transaction ***LT*** - identifies the last processed ***Transaction*** on the ***Deposit smart contract***, which is included in the ***Transfer*** history"},"258":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-60","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#add-tracking-address","content":"`POST /account/{address}` \n`POST /jetton/{jetton}/{address}`"},"259":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-61","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#add-tracking-address","content":"Methods to add a new ***Deposit address*** for tracking. Since a Jetton ***Wallet*** in the TON blockchain is a separate smart contract, when adding a Jetton ***Deposit smart contract*** to tracking, the owner of this wallet (also a smart contract) is added to the tracking as a GRAM ***Deposit smart contract***. The owner of the Jetton wallet pays fees for Jetton withdrawals."},"260":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-62","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#resolve-domain","content":"`GET /resolve/{domain}`"},"261":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-63","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#resolve-domain","content":"Method for resolving domain names on the TON blockchain (presented as NFT), such as `wallet.ton`. Does not work for any domain name, but for a name that has a corresponding DNS record. Returns the address of the contract specified in the DNS record."},"262":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-64","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#status","content":"`GET  /status`"},"263":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-65","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#status","content":"A method for obtaining the current service health and lag when indexing data from the chain."},"264":{"id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed-66","page_id":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/payment-processing/tonapi-embed#methods-for-withdrawals","content":"Methods for constructing and controlling ***withdrawals*** are added upon request based on customer requirements."},"265":{"id":"/tonapi/api-v2","page_id":"/tonapi/api-v2","type":"page","content":"This page was moved","tags":[],"url":"/tonapi/api-v2"},"266":{"id":"/tonapi/api-v2-0","page_id":"/tonapi/api-v2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/tonapi/api-v2","content":"Please visit REST API section for the latest documentation"},"267":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook/emulation","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook/emulation","type":"page","content":"Emulation","breadcrumbs":["Tonconsole","Ton API","Cookbook"],"tags":[],"url":"/tonapi/cookbook/emulation"},"268":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook/emulation-0","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook/emulation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/tonapi/cookbook/emulation","content":"Originating from the Emulation section of the REST API documentation, this recipe provides implementation examples for emulating transactions in TypeScript. Developers can use these examples to integrate emulation into their applications."},"269":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook/emulation-1","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook/emulation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/tonapi/cookbook/emulation","content":"Swagger documentation for Emulation operations can be found here."},"270":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer","type":"page","content":"Gasless Transfer","breadcrumbs":["Tonconsole","Ton API","Cookbook"],"tags":[],"url":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer"},"271":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer-0","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer","content":"Originating from the Gasless section of the REST API documentation, this recipe provides implementation examples for gasless transactions in TypeScript, Go and Python. Developers can use these examples to integrate gasless transactions into their applications."},"272":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer-1","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer","content":"Gasless transactions are currently not supported through TonConnect."},"273":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer-2","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/tonapi/cookbook/gasless-transfer","content":"Swagger documentation for Gasless operations can be found here."},"274":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook","type":"page","content":"Cookbook","breadcrumbs":["Tonconsole","Ton API","Cookbook"],"tags":[],"url":"/tonapi/cookbook"},"275":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook-0","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/tonapi/cookbook#available-recipes","content":"Available Recipes"},"276":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook-1","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/tonapi/cookbook#basic-operations","content":"Basic Operations"},"277":{"id":"/tonapi/cookbook-2","page_id":"/tonapi/cookbook","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/tonapi/cook